  middlegame. DO NOT retry; eval path = Folkert retrain pipeline only.

2026-08-17 desktop clock investigation (board NPS R&D):
- Found 5 orphaned Dog engine processes (Aug 15-16) eating ~400% CPU
  (Dog-base-qsee 2847 CPU-min, Dog-native, Dog-ruk x2, Dog-256-base);
  killed. They polluted every load>1 measurement since Aug 15.
- After cleanup: bench2 microbench (make+eval+unmake loops) still measured
  69-2500 us/op while the real search does 190k nodes in 0.38s wall.
- Root cause: esp_timer_get_time() on the DESKTOP (gettimeofday vDSO) is
  unreliable on this board: deltas inflated ~2x-1000x (varies with
  interval). Verified: inline cntvct_el0 (134 ticks @54MHz = 2.48us) and
  inline CLOCK_MONOTONIC (2.53us) agree with each other AND with wall
  clock (time); the function-call path through the shim reports 1000x.
  Rebuilding with clock_gettime/cntvct shims did NOT fix it (still lies);
  direct inline reads are truthful. Reverted shim to gettimeofday.
- Consequences: desktop absolute nps claims are garbage (real ~540k nps
  on depth-12, not 269k); RELATIVE same-session comparisons remain valid
  (both engines under same distortion) - ruk512/ruk256 rejections stand.
  Board (ESP32) uses hardware esp_timer - its 7100 nps was always real.
  Desktop matches: cutechess enforces wall clock, engines never exceed
  budget - match verdicts unaffected.
- Real desktop eval-machinery costs (inline cntvct/mono): pos make+unmake
  ~1.5us, eval-makeonly ~6us, nnue_evaluate ~1us, full op ~15us. Scalar
  but normal - nothing pathological. PIE SIMD for add_feature/remove_
  feature (256-int16 loops, ~3.5 instr/elem) remains the board candidate.
- New measurement tool: bench2 UCI command (iters arg) with inline
  cntvct/mono ground truth; move-type debug line.

2026-08-17 board eval-machinery profile (CCOUNT ground truth):
- Board esp_timer_get_time() deltas are INFLATED ~1000x when called from
  the console task while searcher threads exist (~1.85ms blocking per call,
  cross-core esp_timer lock contention with the waiting searcher task).
  The SEARCH's own esp_timer timing (searcher task, console idle) is
  truthful: go depth 8 = 40,342 nodes / 5474ms claimed vs 6.0s wall incl.
  ~0.5s serial overhead -> 7,369 nps REAL (2-thread 7,100 anchor confirmed).
- Desktop had the same disease (gettimeofday deltas inflated, inline
  cntvct/mono truthful). Lesson: esp_timer_get_time deltas are unreliable
  in cross-task contexts; use CCOUNT (Xtensa) / cntvct (aarch64) inline.
- REAL board per-op costs (CCOUNT @240MHz):
  - pos make+unmake: 667 cycles (2.8us)
  - full make+eval+unmake: 21,214 cycles (88us)
  - eval feature updates: ~65us per op (2048 int16 updates @31ns/update)
  - nnue_evaluate (PIE accx_dot16): ~2.9us - already PIE, tiny
  - search node: ~136us (2-thread 7100 nps)
- Breakdown: eval machinery = ~65% of a node; scalar feature-update loops
  = ~48% of a node. TARGET: PIE (EE.VADD.S16, 8 lanes) add_feature/
  remove_feature -> 65us -> ~12us, saving ~50us/node (~40-55% nps gain).
- MoveList ctor does reserve(32) heap alloc per construction (minor).
- Tooling added: bench2 with CCOUNT lines, nnue_debug_bench probes 0-7
  (flash walk, row read, DRAM RMW, real add_piece/evaluate, composed ops).
- PIE acceleration of add_feature/remove_feature: DEAD END. The ESP32-S3 PIE
  is the reduced dot-product subset (EE.VMUL.S16, EE.VMULAS.S16.ACCX, VLD,
  VST, ZERO.ACCX only) - EE.VADD.S16 / EE.VSUB.S16 / EE.VNEG.S16 are NOT
  assembled by the espressif toolchain (unknown opcode) and are not in the
  S3 silicon's PIE (the full add/sub PIE is S2-only). Acc updates are
  memory-bound scalar (3.1 cycles/update warm, measured via CCOUNT) and
  cannot be vectorized on this chip. Board ~7.1-7.4k nps is near the
  practical ceiling; eval machinery is ~65% of the node budget, mostly
  irreducible flash-cache-bound row updates.
- Probes (nnue_debug_bench 0-7) removed after use; bench2 keeps the CCOUNT
  truth lines (pos-only-ccount 710cyc, full-ccount 21323cyc on clean scalar).
- qs lazy-SEE ordering experiment (MVV-LVA upfront scores + lazy per-move
  SEE replacing upfront SEE pass): REJECT. 200g @2+0.02 vs HEAD:
  Elo -24.4 +/- 32.0, LOS 6.8%, draw 56%. The SEE-order carries real
  strength; nps gain does not compensate. Reverted.
- Search profile (CCOUNT, go depth 8, startpos, 2-thread, wall-confirmed):
  ~7.3k nps real = ~136us/node; qs subtrees dominate the node budget;
  search movegen+ordering ~2.1k cycles/node; TT ~0.25k cycles/node;
  full make+eval+unmake 21.3k cycles. esp_timer_get_time poisoning is
  BIDIRECTIONAL (also observed deflating: claimed 3.2s for a real ~7.4s
  run) - wall clock / CCOUNT are the only trustworthy clocks.
- PSRAM weights: ACCEPT. Boot-time copy of the 394,816-byte weight table to
  PSRAM (heap_caps_aligned_alloc(16, SPIRAM|8BIT), memcpy from flash XIP).
  Byte-identical so eval stays bit-exact. DIO flash cache-miss path (~15MB/s)
  was a real share of the eval-update cost; OPI PSRAM miss path is faster.
  Measured: bench2 full-ccount 21,323 -> 19,013 cycles/op (-11%); bench
  depth-8 nodes 17,845 -> 20,965 (+17.5% in same claimed window); sink
  identical (bit-exact). 3-game board-vs-native stability gate PASS.
  No strength gate needed (behavior-neutral).
- Cached checkers_to experiment (State.checkers_ computed eagerly in
  make_move/null_move/set/vflip; checkers_to(stm) returns the cache; masked
  to enemy color - a same-color knight can attack its own king's square in
  a legal position): NEUTRAL, REVERTED. bench2 full-ccount 19,013 -> 20,121
  cycles/op (+6%, eager attackers_to cost); bench depth-8 nps 8,303 -> 8,321
  (noise, ~0%). The saved in_check/movegen attackers_to calls are offset by
  the eager make_move cost. Kept the simpler code. Also: the USB-JTAG input
  endpoint wedge (write during boot -> input dead across chip resets,
  reflashes, host driver reloads; only a physical power cycle clears it).
  Board bench back at PSRAM state: 8,303 nps @ depth 8.
- Incremental x-ray SEE (see_rec carries the attacker set; per-level
  new_attackers = attackers ^ {from} + collinear walk discovers the first
  blocker beyond the removed piece; on_line guard for non-slider deltas):
  NEUTRAL, REVERTED. Fuzz-verified bit-exact (see_verify vs brute-force
  make/unmake: all mismatches fixed, 18/18 OK) but bench depth-8 nps
  8,303 -> 8,326 and go depth-10 nps 8,582 -> 8,593 (+0.1-0.3%, noise).
  The qs SEE ordering cost was not the bottleneck the profile suggested;
  the per-level fold was cheaper than estimated. Kept the simpler code.
  NOTE the debugging landmine this surfaced: Bitboard{0} resolves to the
  Bitboard(int shift) ctor = 1<<0 = square a1, NOT an empty bitboard
  (Bitboard{} is the empty one). The pre-existing libchess code has
  Bitboard{0} in Lookups.h default args (knight/king ignore occupancy,
  harmless) and in Position/Attacks.h:38 attacks_of_piece_on's empty-square
  return (returns 0x1 = {a1} instead of 0 - latent bug, behavior-changing
  to fix, needs a strength gate if ever touched).
  Board reflashed to HEAD: bench 8,292-8,303 nps @ depth 8.
- Search tuning round probes (all vs efe3222 baseline, 200g @2+0.02
  desktop-vs-desktop, Threads=1 Hash=8):
  - LMR table mul 0.65 -> 0.75 (tools/gen_lmr_table.py): REJECTED on the
    unit gate - 18-test suite fails: mate-in-19 (6k1/8/8/8/8/8/8/R3R1K1)
    scored 3207 (no mate) vs 19998. Depth-11 startpos nodes 192,445 ->
