    hurts at this NPS - the SEE<0 prune is the right level.
  Both confirm the search parameters sit at a measured local optimum;
  the residual knobs (razor margin multiple, stand-pat skip 350+150d,
  null-move R=2, lmr_start) all have weak or adjacent-rejected priors.
- qs subtree component profile (board CCOUNT, startpos depth-8, 3699 main
  nodes / 24614 qs nodes = 6.65 qs/main): per-qs-node own cost 1959 cycles
  = stand-pat eval 878 (45%), gen_qs_moves_into 487 (25%), SEE scoring 297
  (15%), qs TT probe 292 (15%), selection sort 5 (0.3% - the O(n^2) scan is
  a non-issue, qs breaks early: 5.7 scan steps per qs node). Recursive
  children (make+unmake span 81k cycles incl. the child subtree) = 5875 of
  the 7834 cycles per qs node (75%). CONCLUSION: the qs subtree is at its
  floor - eval is the eval, pruning level was already probed (SEE -40
  rejected), SEE/movegen/TT are cheap. The full 29k cycles/node budget is
  now accounted for (op 19k + movegen/ordering 2.1k + TT 0.25k + qs 7.8k)
  with no reducible components left. NPS R&D complete.
